§ `The accepted spellings of NOT (SET-OPERATORS-SPEC §18.1, R-NOT-1)`

-- The checker refuses `NOT a AND b` and `NOT (a) AND b` on one line
-- (not-ok/tc/not-same-line-connective.l4, not-bracketed-operand.l4). These
-- are the spellings it accepts, evaluated with a = FALSE and b = FALSE so
-- that the two readings give different answers: the narrow reading
-- (NOT a) AND b is FALSE, and the wide reading NOT (a AND b) is TRUE.

-- brackets around the NOT itself: the narrow reading
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
narrow a b MEANS (NOT a) AND b

-- brackets around the whole group: the wide reading, spelled out
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
wide a b MEANS NOT (a AND b)

-- AND on its own line, starting in the NOT's column: it stops the NOT. Narrow.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
stopped a b MEANS
    NOT a
    AND b

-- AND on its own line, further right: the NOT reaches over it. Wide.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
swallowed a b MEANS
    NOT a
        AND b

-- AND on its own line, further left: it stops the NOT. Narrow.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
stoppedLeft a b MEANS
        NOT a
    AND b

-- NOT last on its line: there is nothing after it to reach over
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
lastOnLine a b MEANS a AND NOT b

-- NOT over a bracketed group, then a connective on the NEXT line and further
-- right: the connective is reached over by layout, and that is accepted —
-- it reads as NOT ((a AND b) OR b)
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
groupThenNextLine a b MEANS
    NOT (a AND b)
          OR b

-- UNLESS is `AND NOT` with the NOT written by the parser, not by the
-- reader: no NOT keyword is on the page, so there is nothing to misread and
-- nothing is refused. This is a AND NOT (b AND c).
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
exception a b c MEANS a UNLESS b AND c

-- comparisons are outside the ruling: only one of the two readings is
-- well-typed, so there is nothing to warn about
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
isNotZero n MEANS NOT n EQUALS 0

#EVAL narrow FALSE FALSE
#EVAL wide FALSE FALSE
#EVAL stopped FALSE FALSE
#EVAL swallowed FALSE FALSE
#EVAL stoppedLeft FALSE FALSE
#EVAL lastOnLine TRUE FALSE
#EVAL groupThenNextLine FALSE FALSE
#EVAL exception TRUE TRUE FALSE
#EVAL exception TRUE TRUE TRUE
#EVAL isNotZero 0
#EVAL isNotZero 5

-- the six rows of the ruling's table, as assertions
#ASSERT NOT (narrow FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT wide FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (stopped FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT swallowed FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (stoppedLeft FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT lastOnLine TRUE FALSE
